---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Leaked your Supabase secret key? What to do, step by step"
  description="Which Supabase keys are safe in the browser, which skip every security rule, and how to replace a secret or service role key that leaked."
>
  <article class="guide pt-6">
    <h1>Leaked your Supabase secret key?</h1>
    <p class="lead">Every Supabase project has two kinds of key. One is made for the browser. The other skips every row-level security rule, and if it's in your app's front-end code, anyone who opens your site can copy it.</p>

    <h2>Which key is which</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Where it belongs</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Publishable key, or the older anon key</td><td><code>sb_publishable_…</code>, or a long string starting <code>eyJ</code></td><td>The browser. Your security rules apply to it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Secret key, or the older service role key</td><td><code>sb_secret_…</code>, or a long string starting <code>eyJ</code></td><td>Your server only: edge functions and backend code. It skips every rule.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>If a secret key leaked</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>In the Supabase dashboard, open Settings, then API Keys, and create a new secret key.</li>
      <li>Put the new key wherever your server code used the old one.</li>
      <li>Check that everything works with the new key.</li>
      <li>Delete the old secret key. Deleting can't be undone, so do it last. A leaked older service role key is deactivated in the same place instead.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>If your app runs on Lovable Cloud, the project doesn't appear in a Supabase dashboard: ask in Lovable how to replace its keys.</p>

    <h2>Then move the code that needed it</h2>
    <p>Whatever used the secret key in the browser should run in an edge function, where the key stays on the server and the browser only calls the function.</p>

    <h2>Did GitHub already catch it?</h2>
    <p>Supabase is a GitHub secret scanning partner: older service role keys pushed to a public repository are sent to Supabase, which revokes them and tells you. We couldn't confirm that the newer <code>sb_secret_</code> keys are covered, so replace a leaked key yourself either way.</p>

    <div class="cta">
      <p>Find keys that shouldn't be in your code</p>
      <p>rls-probe looks for both kinds of Supabase secret key, Stripe, OpenAI, Anthropic and AWS keys, and variables like <code>VITE_…SECRET</code> that a build sends to every visitor. <a href={base}>Inspect your app in the browser</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="sources">Sources: Supabase, <a href="https://supabase.com/docs/guides/api/api-keys">Understanding API keys</a>; GitHub, <a href="https://github.blog/changelog/2022-03-28-supabase-is-now-a-github-secret-scanning-partner">Supabase is now a GitHub secret scanning partner</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
